.TH LD 1
.UC 2
.SH NAME
ld \- link editor for the 80286
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ld
[ option ] ... file ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Ld
combines several object files into one program, resolves external
references, and searches libraries.
The output of
.I ld
is left on
.BR a.out ,
which is made executable if no errors occurred during the load.
.PP
The files are loaded in the order specified.
The entry point of the program is the label
.BR ..start ,
which the C library's
.I crt0.o
has (unless the \fB\-e\fP option is specified).
.PP
If any argument is a library, it is searched exactly once
at the point it is encountered in the argument list.
Only those members defining an unresolved external
reference are loaded.
The first member of a library must be a file named `\_\^\_.SYMDEF',
the dictionary for the library that
.IR ranlib (1)
makes;
the dictionary is searched iteratively to satisfy as many references as
possible, so the order of the members is not important.
.PP
A program is segments (see
.IR a.out (5)).
Its data is one: what the files initialize, then their bss, then the
common storage, which may be 64K bytes together with the stack.
In the small model its text is one segment as well, of 64K bytes at
most.
In the medium model, where every function is called far, the text is
as many segments as it takes, 30 at most:
the text of each file is whole in one of them, and files share a
segment, in the order they are loaded, until the next would not fit.
The large model's text is the medium's; its pointers are all far, and
what they point at beyond its data the program is given as it runs.
.PP
The segments after the data are the files' named segments, as a far
variable is in: what every file has of one name is put together, in
the order they are loaded.
The segments of a group are one segment, of 64K bytes at most, and
the others share segments as files share the text.
A program has 31 segments at most.
.PP
A reference to a segment is filled in with its selector, so that the
program is loaded as it is written.
All the files of a program must be for one model.
.PP
The symbols `\_etext', `\_edata' and `\_end'
(`etext', `edata' and `end' in C)
are reserved, and if referred to,
are set to the first location above the last segment of text,
the first location above initialized data,
and the first location above all data respectively.
It is erroneous to define these symbols.
.PP
.I Ld
understands several options.
Except for
.BR \-l ,
they may appear anywhere among the file names.
.TP
.B \-b
Make an image, as a program that is started where it is loaded by
something other than
.IR execve (2)
is, such as
.IR /boot :
its text, its data and then its bss and common storage in one segment
of the small model, and in the file the bytes of the text and the data
and nothing else.
Its addresses are counted from its first byte, where it is started,
which must be its entry point if it has one; and nothing in it may be
a selector.
.TP
.B \-e
The following argument is taken to be the
name of the entry point of the loaded
program.
.TP
.BI \-gdt " n"
The selectors are of the global descriptor table, segment 1 in slot
.I n
and the others after it, and not of a process's own table: for the
kernel.
.TP
.BI \-L dir
Add
.I dir
to the list of directories in which libraries are searched for.
Directories specified with
.B \-L
are searched before the standard directories.
.TP
.BI \-l x
This
option is an abbreviation for the library name
.RI `lib x .a',
where
.I x
is a string.
.I Ld
searches for libraries first in any directories
specified with
.B \-L
options, then in the standard directories `/lib', `/usr/lib', and
`/usr/local/lib', or for the medium model in the directory `m' of
each of those, and for the large in `l'.
A library is searched when its name is encountered,
so the placement of a
.B  \-l
is significant.
.TP
.B \-M
produce a primitive load map: each global symbol with its segment
and its address, each segment with its size, and where each named
segment is; or, of an image, how big it is in memory and in the file.
.TP
.B \-ml
Make a program of the large model.
.TP
.B \-mm
Make a program of the medium model.
.TP
.B \-ms
Make a program of the small model, which is the default.
.TP
.B \-o
The
.I name
argument after
.B \-o
is used as the name of the
.I ld
output file, instead of
.BR a.out .
.TP
.BI \-packcode " n"
In the medium and large models, put no more than
.I n
bytes of text in a segment, where 65536 is the default.
.TP
.B  \-s
`strip' the output, that is, remove the symbol table
to save space (but impair the
usefulness of the debuggers).
This information can also be removed by
.IR  strip (1).
.TP
.BI \-stack " n"
The program is to have a stack of
.I n
bytes, where 8192 is the default.
.TP
.B \-t
("trace")  Print the name of each file as it is processed.
.TP
.B  \-u
Take the following argument as a symbol and enter
it as undefined in the symbol table.  This is useful
for loading wholly from a library, since initially the symbol
table is empty and an unresolved reference is needed
to force the loading of the first routine.
.TP
.B  \-x
Do not preserve local
(non-global) symbols in the output symbol table; only enter
external symbols.
This option saves some space in the output file.
.B \-X
is the same: the assembler leaves the compiler's own labels out of
an object.
.PP
The PDP-11's
.BR \-i ,
.B \-n
and
.B \-z
are accepted and do nothing: every program here has its text apart
from its data, and shares it.
.SH FILES
.ta \w'/usr/local/lib/lib*.a\ \ 'u
/lib/lib*.a	libraries
.br
/usr/lib/lib*.a	more libraries
.br
/usr/local/lib/lib*.a	still more libraries
.br
/lib/m/lib*.a	the same for the medium model, and so on
.br
/lib/crt0.o	what a C program starts with
.br
a.out	output file
.SH "SEE ALSO"
ar(1), as(1), cc(1), file(1), nm(1), ranlib(1),
size(1), strip(1), a.out(5)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
`Undefined:' and the names of the symbols that no file defines.
`\fIname\fP is called near from another segment':
in the medium model, a file refers to a function in another file's
text other than by a far call or a far pointer.
.SH BUGS
There is no
.BR \-r :
the output cannot be the input of another
.I ld
run.
.PP
A file's text is never divided between segments, so one file of the
medium model can have no more than 64K bytes of it.
